#{[#edb/ref 17592186050905 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715} :activity/id #uuid "a9b4379c-c4ae-5316-aee1-bbb38b252dff" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102032 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0)=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n- separating the variables,\n\n- integrating the equation, and then\n\n- substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100732 :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101044}] [#edb/ref 17592186050869 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483} :activity/id #uuid "3a675a3e-a62b-5b11-9c31-522dea85c72d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740}] [#edb/ref 17592186050885 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180447} :activity/id #uuid "563e73f4-5c65-5c01-b1d4-1909214d3b23" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180700 :tutorial/content "Recall that  if $f(x,y)$ is a function whose partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},\n$$\n\nexist and are continuous at a point $(x_0,y_0) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the gradient of $f$ evaluated at $(x_0, y_0)$ is given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial x}(x_0, y_0) \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial y} (x_0, y_0)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\, .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow suppose that the gradient vector exists at every point $(x,y)$ contained within the set $U\\subset \\mathbb R^2.$ Then, the **gradient vector field** of $f$ is defined as the map\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\longmapsto \\nabla f(x,y), \\qquad (x,y)\\in U.\n$$\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.$ To find the gradient vector field of $f,$ we proceed as follows.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this vector field is shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7437bd110810a0356a25afea897634c152b0441aee8e6403aa051dd2c1f205a7.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/f9/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3d/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/99/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179581 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180705 :tutorial/content "Consider the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y,$ shown below. How is this vector field related to the level curves of $f?$\n\n![](images/ef/ef5800723603b78beeb40c4352153f37298d4836905a6d8b751a919924664fa5.png)\n\nTo answer this question, we note the following:\n\n- As we've seen, the gradient of $f$ at a particular point $(x_0, y_0)$ lies *perpendicular* to the level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0, y_0).$\n\n- Therefore, the gradient field vectors of $f$ at an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ lie perpendicular to its level curve at $(x,y).$\n\n- Moreover, the vectors always point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ is increasing.\n\nNow, if we superimpose the level curves of $f$ onto its vector field, we get a picture that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5bd2729b82312a50ee0c274fc1591545588f4881e2e279e80aca6b68f76ee824.png)\n\nNotice that the field vectors are indeed perpendicular to the level curves.\n\nWe can use a similar idea to sketch the vector field of a function using its level curves. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180448}}] :activity/title "Gradient Vector Fields" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179846}] [#edb/ref 17592186050884 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035} :activity/id #uuid "de17f2e1-7eda-5a05-b05f-fc9631097dd2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205174 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\n- $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\n- $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205208 :tutorial/content "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have No Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**\n\nSimilarly, the equation\n\n\\[\n(p + 3)x = -9,\n\\]\n\nwhere [math]p[/math] is a constant, is an equation in the form [math]ax = b{:}[/math] \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(p + 3)}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-9}_{\\large b},\n\\]\n\nIn this case,\n\n\\[\na = p + 3, \\qquad b = -9.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\\[\np + 3 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving this for [math]p,[/math] we get\n\\begin{align*}\np + 3 & = 0 \\\\[5pt]\np & = -3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the equation has no solution when [math]p=-3.[/math]" :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/id #uuid "5ee14499-89b0-59a3-9f13-b0a00c0e518d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ {{(r-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/id #uuid "36eadc0b-c2b0-5f84-8f39-2b8399932edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-17214" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204177}]}
